          "text": "# Beta Phe: A Steady Light in the Phoenix\n\n*An orange-tinged giant anchors a quiet corner of the southern sky\u2014and rewards observers who give it time to rise.*\n\nOn a clear spring evening in the Southern Hemisphere, look south for Phoenix, a constellation shaped less like a bird than a scattered trail of modest stars. Beta Phe is one of its brighter markers: a point of light that can be picked out with the unaided eye, but becomes more interesting when you pause over it. In SIMBAD\u2019s catalogue, the star is listed at visual magnitude 3.30 and classified as spectral type G8III\u2014a giant star. [SIMBAD: Beta Phe](https://simbad.cds.unistra.fr/simbad/sim-id?Ident=HIP%205165)\n\nThe \u201cIII\u201d in that classification is the clue to its life stage. Beta Phe is not a Sun-like star simply shining from a familiar, steady middle age: it has evolved into a giant. Its G8 classification places it in the cooler part of the G-star range, though a star\u2019s apparent color can be subtle to the eye. Compare it with nearby stars, then look away and back; sometimes the impression of warmth emerges only after a few moments.\n\nThe catalogue also calls Beta Phe a \u201cdouble or multiple star.\u201d That description is an invitation to investigate, not a promise that two points will be obvious in a small telescope. The entry supplied here does not give a component separation, so don\u2019t assume the system can be split visually. Begin with binoculars or the naked eye, and enjoy the star as a bright point set against Phoenix\u2019s comparatively sparse field. A telescope at low power can help you explore the surrounding stars, even if Beta itself remains unresolved.\n\nThere is a useful caution in the numbers. SIMBAD lists a parallax of 0.12 milliarcseconds with an uncertainty of 14.62 milliarcseconds. Because the uncertainty is vastly larger than the measured value, that entry does not provide a dependable distance. It is a reminder that catalogues 